---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import dark from '../assets/decks/default-dark.png';
import glass from '../assets/decks/glass.png';
import light from '../assets/decks/default-light.png';

const decks = [
  { src: glass, alt: 'A deck with the glass theme over a moving aurora background: a split-flap clock, weather with a five-day forecast, and gauges for CPU, GPU and memory.', caption: 'glass theme over the aurora background' },
  { src: dark, alt: 'The same deck with the default dark theme: a clock, weather, gauges, and four shortcuts in the dock.', caption: 'default-dark' },
  { src: light, alt: 'The same deck with the default light theme over a soft gradient background.', caption: 'default-light over a gradient' },
];
---
<Base title="UrDeck" description="A lightweight widget dashboard for a secondary or case display on Windows. No account, no telemetry, free software.">
  <section class="hero">
    <div>
      <h1>A small dashboard for the screen beside your screen.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        UrDeck puts clocks, system readings, weather and shortcuts on a secondary or case display. It is a native Windows
        app drawn on the GPU, and it redraws only what changed, so a page with nothing moving costs almost nothing.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="https://github.com/UrDeck/urdeck">Download: coming soon</a>
        <a class="button" href="https://github.com/UrDeck/urdeck">Source on GitHub</a>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        There is no download yet: a release will come once its files can be signed, so Windows can tell they are mine. Until
        then the button leads to the repository, where UrDeck can be built from source.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="deck-hero">
      <Image class="deck-img" src={glass} alt={decks[0].alt} widths={[320, 480, 640]} sizes="(max-width: 52rem) 20rem, 28rem" loading="eager" />
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>I wanted the small screen in my case to show things without a program behind it that I cannot trust or see into.</p>
    <ol class="reasons">
      <li><strong>Private.</strong> No account, no telemetry. UrDeck makes two kinds of network request, both for something you put on the page: a forecast for a place you typed into a weather card, and the icon of a web address you gave a shortcut. Nothing else leaves your machine, and a local icon file avoids even that.</li>
      <li><strong>Light.</strong> It is a native app that draws each widget on the GPU and redraws only what changed. A moving background is optional and has a setting to make it cheaper.</li>
      <li><strong>Yours.</strong> One JSON file, themes you can write in a few lines, backgrounds written as small shaders. Widgets are plain C# plugins that load and reload while UrDeck runs. Sharing widgets, backgrounds and themes through a gallery is planned, not built.</li>
      <li><strong>Open.</strong> The source is on GitHub under the GPL; the plugin SDK is MIT, so a widget can use any licence.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Sample decks</h2>
    <p>
      These are pictures made by UrDeck itself, from config files kept in the repository, at the size of the panel it was
      made for (1100 by 3840). The readings and the time are those of the machine that drew them.
    </p>
    <div class="decks">
      {decks.map((d) => (
        <figure>
          <Image class="deck-img" src={d.src} alt={d.alt} widths={[240, 360, 480]} sizes="(max-width: 52rem) 90vw, 20rem" />
          <figcaption>{d.caption}</figcaption>
        </figure>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Go further</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/docs/">Configure it</a>: the config file, pages, the dock, each widget, themes and backgrounds.</li>
      <li><a href="/tested-on/">Tested on</a>: the hardware it has run on, and how to report yours.</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/UrDeck/urdeck">The repository</a>: source, roadmap and issues.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
